\title{Some results in constructive cardinal theory}
\taxon{Talk}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\import{common}
\date{2026-08-12}
\meta{venue}{Geometry and Topology Seminar, [Western University](uwo); Invited talk}
\meta{source}{[Calendar](https://mathcal.sci.uwo.ca/V3/search.php)}
\meta{slides}{\asset-url{assets/talks/western-2026-slides.pdf}}

\abstract{
\p{
While the basic study of cardinals are undeniably important in mathematics, they are
surprisingly ill-behaved in the constructive setting, where they are currently poorly
understood. In this seminar, I will present a series of novel constructive results on
cardinals, including a constructivization of the Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein theorem, a
predicative proof of the uncountability of the (MacNeille) reals, and, if time
permits, a constructivization of König’s theorem. Along the way, I will highlight
aspects and challenges in the study of constructive cardinals, as well as some ways
to tackle them.
}
}
